WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.530 --> 00:00:02.210
Welcome back to
Academy Dialogues.

00:00:02.210 --> 00:00:05.000
This is our final episode this
season and the conversation

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:08.000
today is Broadening the
Aperture of Excellence.

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:10.940
This conversation is moderated
by filmmaker, changemaker,

00:00:10.940 --> 00:00:13.370
and Academy Governor,
Ava DuVernay.

00:00:13.370 --> 00:00:15.810
Joining Ava, two inspiring
and highly regarded

00:00:15.810 --> 00:00:18.760
filmmakers, Julie
Dash and Euzhan Palcy.

00:00:18.760 --> 00:00:20.090
On to you, Ava.

00:00:20.090 --> 00:00:20.840
Thank you, Shawn.

00:00:20.840 --> 00:00:24.050
Thank you to the Academy for
giving us the time, space,

00:00:24.050 --> 00:00:27.740
the platform to talk,
and for some of you

00:00:27.740 --> 00:00:32.570
learn more about two truly
extraordinary filmmakers.

00:00:32.570 --> 00:00:35.692
These are my cinematic
heroes, and I'm so honored--

00:00:35.692 --> 00:00:38.150
I feel emotional, even been
saying it and being in the room

00:00:38.150 --> 00:00:40.730
with you both, and
it's a virtual room.

00:00:40.730 --> 00:00:43.160
This is crazy, but
I'll take it, because I

00:00:43.160 --> 00:00:44.280
love you both so much.

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:46.160
So many people
always talk about,

00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:48.240
Ava, you're the first
this, first that,

00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:53.120
and I never lose an opportunity
to talk about the women

00:00:53.120 --> 00:00:56.180
who not only came before,
but who I had the opportunity

00:00:56.180 --> 00:00:57.140
to work beside.

00:00:57.140 --> 00:00:59.660
Because these women
are dynamic directors

00:00:59.660 --> 00:01:03.740
who were working long before
I was, and who still are.

00:01:03.740 --> 00:01:07.220
And so I'm so pleased to
have Miss Dash, Miss Palcy,

00:01:07.220 --> 00:01:10.790
the queens, here with us today.

00:01:10.790 --> 00:01:13.310
And our real goal
here is for people

00:01:13.310 --> 00:01:18.950
who don't know about the
dynamic, lush, delicious,

00:01:18.950 --> 00:01:22.280
acclaimed work of the two
of you to get to know it.

00:01:22.280 --> 00:01:25.400
And so often when I hear
you all interviewed,

00:01:25.400 --> 00:01:27.530
it's talking from
a sense of lack,

00:01:27.530 --> 00:01:31.200
of absence, all the
things that happened,

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:33.290
all the hardships
within the industry.

00:01:33.290 --> 00:01:36.080
And what I want to do is
let this be a real filmmaker

00:01:36.080 --> 00:01:39.770
conversation about craft, about
the way that you see your work

00:01:39.770 --> 00:01:44.000
as artists and about your
mastery of the craft,

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:46.160
because you both are masters.

00:01:46.160 --> 00:01:50.260
So we shall begin
with two Black girls

00:01:50.260 --> 00:01:53.240
born in the '50's,
one in Martinique

00:01:53.240 --> 00:01:55.490
and one in New York City.

00:01:55.490 --> 00:02:00.290
Here they are, and I want to
know, first films you saw?

00:02:00.290 --> 00:02:04.730
How did you first start
to fall in love with film?

00:02:04.730 --> 00:02:08.839
I know Euzhan, you would go
to the matinees in Martinique.

00:02:08.839 --> 00:02:10.880
Can you talk a little
bit about how you

00:02:10.880 --> 00:02:15.430
started to come to love movies?

00:02:15.430 --> 00:02:16.940
That's right.

00:02:16.940 --> 00:02:21.130
We used to go to the--

00:02:21.130 --> 00:02:27.100
priest had a room that
he turned into a theater,

00:02:27.100 --> 00:02:33.850
and we would go to see
movies after the big service

00:02:33.850 --> 00:02:37.120
after 11:00 AM, and
our parents would

00:02:37.120 --> 00:02:41.690
go in the evening to see a
different kind of movies.

00:02:41.690 --> 00:02:44.200
So that's where I was
able to discoverer

00:02:44.200 --> 00:02:49.580
of for example like a
Black Orpheus from Camus.

00:02:49.580 --> 00:02:54.940
And I saw, for the first time,
Black people on screen kissing,

00:02:54.940 --> 00:02:58.330
and loving, and children.

00:02:58.330 --> 00:03:02.950
And I was so happy, because
I'd been so starving

00:03:02.950 --> 00:03:08.980
and frustrated with not
seeing our people on screen.

00:03:08.980 --> 00:03:10.980
And I saw others of color.

00:03:10.980 --> 00:03:15.000
Of course, but you're
in the priest's room.

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:19.250
So you've gone to
mass, then you went

00:03:19.250 --> 00:03:24.170
into the priest's back room
and watched matinee movies.

00:03:24.170 --> 00:03:29.780
And could you have ever
imagined your path then?

00:03:29.780 --> 00:03:33.890
Can you give us
the summary of how

00:03:33.890 --> 00:03:35.930
you got from that girl
in the priest's room

00:03:35.930 --> 00:03:40.670
watching Black Orpheus to
the set of Sugar Cane Alley.

00:03:40.670 --> 00:03:43.080
What came in between?

00:03:43.080 --> 00:03:48.100
Well, what happened was that
I decided to make films,

00:03:48.100 --> 00:03:49.870
I was 10 years old.

00:03:49.870 --> 00:03:53.580
Because we were going to
the movies all the time,

00:03:53.580 --> 00:04:00.090
and most of those films would
come from Europe or America.

00:04:00.090 --> 00:04:04.270
As I said earlier, I was
always forcefully not seeing

00:04:04.270 --> 00:04:05.800
any Black faces, people like me.

00:04:08.470 --> 00:04:11.850
So after I would see a movie,
I would question my parents.

00:04:11.850 --> 00:04:15.910
I would say, but how come
we are not on the screen,

00:04:15.910 --> 00:04:19.180
we are absent?

00:04:19.180 --> 00:04:21.990
My mother would
say, I don't know.

00:04:21.990 --> 00:04:25.960
But I would question
her so much, so much,

00:04:25.960 --> 00:04:29.050
they would call me
Mademoiselle Why--

00:04:29.050 --> 00:04:30.350
Miss Why.

00:04:30.350 --> 00:04:32.096
[CHUCKLING]

00:04:32.096 --> 00:04:35.260
And my grandmother would
say to me, you know what?

00:04:35.260 --> 00:04:39.970
I'll give you 20 minutes if you
want to-- you complain about

00:04:39.970 --> 00:04:40.750
things--

00:04:40.750 --> 00:04:42.730
but I'll give you 10
minutes to tell me

00:04:42.730 --> 00:04:44.772
how you're going to change
this, what do you want

00:04:44.772 --> 00:04:46.250
to do to it makes a difference?

00:04:46.250 --> 00:04:47.930
And she was talking
like that to me.

00:04:47.930 --> 00:04:50.200
And one day my mother
wanted me to get out

00:04:50.200 --> 00:04:53.350
of her space in the
kitchen, because I

00:04:53.350 --> 00:04:55.150
kept questioning, questioning.

00:04:55.150 --> 00:04:59.800
And she gave me a book, the
book is Sugar Cane Alley.

00:04:59.800 --> 00:05:03.730
In French is La Rue
Cases-Nègres, Sugar Cane Alley.

00:05:03.730 --> 00:05:07.700
Sit down, read that book, and
please, read every single page.

00:05:07.700 --> 00:05:10.240
Don't flip the pages, because
I want to do the story,

00:05:10.240 --> 00:05:12.040
I know it by heart myself.

00:05:12.040 --> 00:05:15.730
But she didn't know
that simple gesture

00:05:15.730 --> 00:05:19.900
to get her peace, that
gesture which in her own life,

00:05:19.900 --> 00:05:24.640
because it was just like
a hurricane in my body.

00:05:24.640 --> 00:05:27.580
And I saw my people for
the first time in a book--

00:05:27.580 --> 00:05:32.120
Black folks, children, old
people, Africa, Africans.

00:05:32.120 --> 00:05:35.140
And I said, oh, I want to
make a movie with that,

00:05:35.140 --> 00:05:38.670
and that stuck with me
for years and years.

00:05:38.670 --> 00:05:42.292
And that's how-- it was
a struggle after that

00:05:42.292 --> 00:05:43.750
to get the money
to make the movie,

00:05:43.750 --> 00:05:45.390
but that's another thing.

00:05:45.390 --> 00:05:47.945
That's another story.

00:05:47.945 --> 00:05:49.570
It's always a struggle
to make a movie,

00:05:49.570 --> 00:05:51.940
but I love the fact that you
describe it as a hurricane that

00:05:51.940 --> 00:05:52.810
happened within you.

00:05:52.810 --> 00:05:58.545
And anybody who has seen Miss
Palcy's classic, seminal Sugar

00:05:58.545 --> 00:06:01.570
Cane Alley then you've missed
out, but you can fix it.

00:06:01.570 --> 00:06:04.660
Go find it, and watch her work.

00:06:04.660 --> 00:06:07.030
Miss Dash, Queen Dash--

00:06:07.030 --> 00:06:12.040
New York City, rough
and tumble, that's

00:06:12.040 --> 00:06:14.100
what I think of New York City.

00:06:14.100 --> 00:06:16.300
Queensbridge Projects,
that's right.

00:06:16.300 --> 00:06:19.120
How did you first
start to see films?

00:06:19.120 --> 00:06:20.320
And at what age?

00:06:20.320 --> 00:06:22.210
Younger or older?

00:06:22.210 --> 00:06:23.290
I was very young.

00:06:23.290 --> 00:06:27.150
I just remember there
was the Beacon Theater

00:06:27.150 --> 00:06:29.380
was the only theater
in Queensbridge

00:06:29.380 --> 00:06:31.030
Projects across the
street from where

00:06:31.030 --> 00:06:34.540
I grew up on Vernon Boulevard.

00:06:34.540 --> 00:06:39.515
I used to go to the movies
and fall asleep all the time.

00:06:39.515 --> 00:06:40.890
Movies were
different then, there

00:06:40.890 --> 00:06:44.050
were two movies
plus ten cartoons.

00:06:44.050 --> 00:06:47.320
And they would have to wake me
up when it was time to leave.

00:06:47.320 --> 00:06:50.590
I had really no plans
of being a filmmaker,

00:06:50.590 --> 00:06:53.800
I didn't even know
how films were made.

00:06:53.800 --> 00:06:56.560
I thought the actors were
making up the lines as they were

00:06:56.560 --> 00:06:58.750
going along, I didn't know.

00:06:58.750 --> 00:07:06.480
But Black Orpheus-- I remember
I saw it for the first time

00:07:06.480 --> 00:07:08.340
on television.

00:07:08.340 --> 00:07:10.950
My mother woke my sister
and myself up from sleep

00:07:10.950 --> 00:07:13.470
and said, hurry up,
come see the television,

00:07:13.470 --> 00:07:16.600
Black people are on television,
and it was Black Orpheus.

00:07:16.600 --> 00:07:17.100
Wow.

00:07:17.100 --> 00:07:20.610
And it was late at
night, and it was dubbed.

00:07:20.610 --> 00:07:23.710
And I realized that I'd never
seen a dubbed film before,

00:07:23.710 --> 00:07:26.910
so it was like, hmm, a little,
something is a little strange.

00:07:26.910 --> 00:07:29.462
But everyone was like really
hot and moving around,

00:07:29.462 --> 00:07:31.670
and there were Black people,
and they were beautiful,

00:07:31.670 --> 00:07:32.587
and they were dancing.

00:07:32.587 --> 00:07:35.080
And it was like, oh, that's
just like the people I know.

00:07:35.080 --> 00:07:37.760
But it's kind of strange, it's
something with the voices.

00:07:37.760 --> 00:07:39.650
[CHUCKLING]

00:07:39.650 --> 00:07:41.910
It was hyper reality.

00:07:41.910 --> 00:07:45.850
But it was Black Orpheus,
and that stuck with me.

00:07:45.850 --> 00:07:48.780
And to this day, it remains
one of my favorite films.

00:07:48.780 --> 00:07:54.870
But let's leap frog this, in
1968, I was in high school,

00:07:54.870 --> 00:07:59.640
and I stumbled into a
filmmaking workshop in Harlem

00:07:59.640 --> 00:08:02.380
called the Studio
Museum of Harlem.

00:08:02.380 --> 00:08:04.110
They had a
cinematography workshop.

00:08:04.110 --> 00:08:07.890
We were there for something
like two weeks, my best friend,

00:08:07.890 --> 00:08:10.410
Wembe, and I. We were
there for about two weeks

00:08:10.410 --> 00:08:13.290
before we realized it wasn't
a still photography class,

00:08:13.290 --> 00:08:16.050
it was cinematography.

00:08:16.050 --> 00:08:18.990
So by that time we were hooked.

00:08:18.990 --> 00:08:25.980
We were watching Jules and Jim,
and all these foreign films,

00:08:25.980 --> 00:08:33.059
Potemkin, Eistenstein's
Potemkin, and Tokyo Story.

00:08:33.059 --> 00:08:36.659
And I was just
hooked at that point.

00:08:36.659 --> 00:08:40.720
And pretty much like Euzhan
was saying, at that point,

00:08:40.720 --> 00:08:44.590
I started wondering, why are
the films so much better?

00:08:44.590 --> 00:08:48.870
The foreign films, why can't
we see ourselves on the screen?

00:08:48.870 --> 00:08:53.030
Why can't we see
renditions of Sula,

00:08:53.030 --> 00:08:56.640
or The Bluest Eye, Toni
Morrison's work, Alice Walker's

00:08:56.640 --> 00:09:00.370
work, Gayl--

00:09:00.370 --> 00:09:03.630
the woman who wrote Corregidora,
I forget her last name.

00:09:03.630 --> 00:09:06.840
But that was pretty
much, by the time

00:09:06.840 --> 00:09:10.830
I got to college, that's why I
wanted to become a filmmaker,

00:09:10.830 --> 00:09:13.110
so I could start
telling those stories

00:09:13.110 --> 00:09:15.640
and learning how to
tell those stories.

00:09:15.640 --> 00:09:21.390
So then you and your feature
narrative directorial debut

00:09:21.390 --> 00:09:25.990
is Daughters of the Dust.

00:09:25.990 --> 00:09:28.140
The long form.

00:09:28.140 --> 00:09:31.274
But I had made
quite a few films.

00:09:31.274 --> 00:09:34.290
You had made quite a few, that's
why I asked it as a question,

00:09:34.290 --> 00:09:37.110
because it's the featured,
narrative directorial debut was

00:09:37.110 --> 00:09:39.000
Daughters of the Dust.

00:09:39.000 --> 00:09:43.270
It just happened to
be a lifelong classic,

00:09:43.270 --> 00:09:45.180
this is the first
one, it's fine.

00:09:45.180 --> 00:09:52.010
And so the two of
you are making films,

00:09:52.010 --> 00:09:55.290
and debuting your
cinematic voices

00:09:55.290 --> 00:10:00.210
to the world around the same
time, within a four or five

00:10:00.210 --> 00:10:02.353
year window of one another.

00:10:02.353 --> 00:10:04.770
And so I wanted to just talk
a little bit about that time,

00:10:04.770 --> 00:10:08.070
the late '80's, early '90's.

00:10:08.070 --> 00:10:10.980
And I look at old pictures
of the two of you,

00:10:10.980 --> 00:10:15.660
and I have pored over pictures
of the two of you on your sets

00:10:15.660 --> 00:10:18.000
and all of your
publicity photos,

00:10:18.000 --> 00:10:19.920
and I've read every interview.

00:10:19.920 --> 00:10:21.570
But I just want you
to take us back,

00:10:21.570 --> 00:10:24.450
because there were
there was so few of you,

00:10:24.450 --> 00:10:28.080
and yet there were
a lot more than it

00:10:28.080 --> 00:10:30.930
had been all in that time.

00:10:30.930 --> 00:10:34.380
I think you can count
about a dozen filmmakers

00:10:34.380 --> 00:10:39.630
around that time that
were making work of note.

00:10:39.630 --> 00:10:42.650
And yet, only a
handful of women.

00:10:42.650 --> 00:10:45.510
So how that felt being
at that renaissance,

00:10:45.510 --> 00:10:47.650
that Black renaissance
of film '80's,

00:10:47.650 --> 00:10:52.020
early '90's, and what the
point of view of women

00:10:52.020 --> 00:10:53.460
with the next circle was?

00:10:53.460 --> 00:10:55.170
Did you all know each other?

00:10:55.170 --> 00:10:56.700
Were you all talking
to each other?

00:10:56.700 --> 00:10:58.410
Were you supporting each other?

00:10:58.410 --> 00:11:01.650
What-- were the Americans
in touch with filmmakers who

00:11:01.650 --> 00:11:02.880
were outside of the country?

00:11:02.880 --> 00:11:05.120
How was it?

00:11:05.120 --> 00:11:07.490
Well, I met Euzhan at--

00:11:07.490 --> 00:11:10.730
PBS had a retreat in Utah.

00:11:10.730 --> 00:11:13.050
Do you remember that,
the Sundance Retreat?

00:11:13.050 --> 00:11:13.550
Yes.

00:11:13.550 --> 00:11:15.740
That was the first time
I met her in person,

00:11:15.740 --> 00:11:18.920
but I knew her
films before then.

00:11:18.920 --> 00:11:22.970
Of course, Rue Cases Négres
was one of my favorites,

00:11:22.970 --> 00:11:25.620
because it's a foreign film.

00:11:25.620 --> 00:11:28.370
So I knew her.

00:11:28.370 --> 00:11:33.770
And I started traveling to
various international film

00:11:33.770 --> 00:11:40.280
festivals at a very young
age with my short films.

00:11:40.280 --> 00:11:44.540
We went to London,
France, Munich, Venice,

00:11:44.540 --> 00:11:45.780
all of these places.

00:11:45.780 --> 00:11:50.600
And so we were interacting with
African filmmakers in France.

00:11:50.600 --> 00:11:57.470
Med Hondo, Idrissa,
[INAUDIBLE],, all of these people

00:11:57.470 --> 00:12:01.520
long before I made
Daughters of the Dust.

00:12:01.520 --> 00:12:04.220
OK.

00:12:04.220 --> 00:12:08.450
Wow, and Euzhan, how
do you remember it?

00:12:08.450 --> 00:12:13.160
You know what, I saw
Daughters of the Dust

00:12:13.160 --> 00:12:16.070
when I came to America.

00:12:16.070 --> 00:12:19.320
Because American
film would not go

00:12:19.320 --> 00:12:23.450
to Europe for the simple
reason that, even the studios

00:12:23.450 --> 00:12:28.370
today, they are telling us that
when you win a project, well,

00:12:28.370 --> 00:12:33.170
you know, we won't be
able to market this,

00:12:33.170 --> 00:12:36.350
foreign won't like
it, they won't buy it.

00:12:36.350 --> 00:12:40.550
And it's complicated, because
the leads are Black or females

00:12:40.550 --> 00:12:42.130
don't even talk about that.

00:12:44.960 --> 00:12:49.610
So for that reason, there
were very few Americans--

00:12:49.610 --> 00:12:53.940
American films with Black
folks, Black heroes.

00:12:53.940 --> 00:12:59.570
That piece is a beautiful
piece, a masterpiece.

00:12:59.570 --> 00:13:01.670
I've already told you
about how beautiful

00:13:01.670 --> 00:13:07.510
that movie was, the people, the
directing, and the photography,

00:13:07.510 --> 00:13:10.010
it was fantastic.

00:13:10.010 --> 00:13:15.560
I was talking about it with
my friend, Jean-Pierre,

00:13:15.560 --> 00:13:18.550
because he doesn't
know the film,

00:13:18.550 --> 00:13:23.500
and I will make sure
that you see that movie.

00:13:23.500 --> 00:13:29.370
And then when we met, I was
just like, [GASP],, of course.

00:13:29.370 --> 00:13:35.450
Because I was so
lonely, and that's

00:13:35.450 --> 00:13:38.390
hard when you are like that.

00:13:38.390 --> 00:13:41.590
When you are the only one, you
have nobody like you around,

00:13:41.590 --> 00:13:44.970
and when we met, it was great.

00:13:44.970 --> 00:13:47.930
Wow, that's so interesting
that concept of loneliness.

00:13:47.930 --> 00:13:50.150
And I think I just
want to talk about that

00:13:50.150 --> 00:13:57.170
in a context of the women
who influenced you and came

00:13:57.170 --> 00:13:57.740
before you.

00:13:57.740 --> 00:14:01.280
And I know from talking to you
and having the honor of you

00:14:01.280 --> 00:14:04.180
both spending time
with me personally,

00:14:04.180 --> 00:14:07.790
that you both have quite the
connection to Kathleen Collins.

00:14:07.790 --> 00:14:10.070
And so I wanted to take
a little bit of time

00:14:10.070 --> 00:14:14.120
in this space to talk about
our beautiful sister, Kathleen

00:14:14.120 --> 00:14:15.260
Collins.

00:14:15.260 --> 00:14:20.840
And for people that do not
know, regrettably, who she was

00:14:20.840 --> 00:14:23.900
and what she left
behind, can you

00:14:23.900 --> 00:14:26.510
talk a little bit about
how you knew her and bit

00:14:26.510 --> 00:14:27.750
about her work?

00:14:27.750 --> 00:14:31.260
Euzhan, can you start us off?

00:14:31.260 --> 00:14:41.660
Yeah, I was in Boston,
and she invited me there

00:14:41.660 --> 00:14:43.420
with my film, Sugar Cane Alley.

00:14:47.230 --> 00:14:50.890
And there was a screening,
a documentary that they

00:14:50.890 --> 00:14:56.580
did about her, but she was
dead, she died a few years

00:14:56.580 --> 00:15:00.070
before that screening.

00:15:00.070 --> 00:15:03.380
I didn't know
anything about her,

00:15:03.380 --> 00:15:05.840
and there it is, I'm
sitting there, watching

00:15:05.840 --> 00:15:09.480
that woman, that
passionate woman,

00:15:09.480 --> 00:15:13.190
and talking about acting.

00:15:13.190 --> 00:15:20.330
She had [INAUDIBLE],, and
also, she was so angry,

00:15:20.330 --> 00:15:25.340
because she wanted to do
more, and Hollywood would not

00:15:25.340 --> 00:15:29.750
give her what she would need
just to make her feature films.

00:15:29.750 --> 00:15:32.270
And she was talking about
all kinds of things.

00:15:36.050 --> 00:15:39.690
When I was watching her
and listening to her,

00:15:39.690 --> 00:15:43.650
I recognized myself in her--

00:15:43.650 --> 00:15:48.083
my passion, the
fight I had to put on

00:15:48.083 --> 00:15:51.190
to do Sugar Cane Alley
and all of these things.

00:15:51.190 --> 00:15:53.850
And I started to cry
when I understood

00:15:53.850 --> 00:15:59.610
that she died from
cancer, and young, she

00:15:59.610 --> 00:16:02.430
was 40 or something like that.

00:16:02.430 --> 00:16:03.540
Yeah.

00:16:03.540 --> 00:16:07.650
Oh my God, I started to cry,
and I'm saying that, I have oof.

00:16:07.650 --> 00:16:12.810
And at the end of the
screening at that festival,

00:16:12.810 --> 00:16:17.245
I decided to look for
her husband, Alfred,

00:16:17.245 --> 00:16:18.870
and he told me that
he was in New York,

00:16:18.870 --> 00:16:21.090
and they gave me the
address, and I called him.

00:16:21.090 --> 00:16:23.760
And I said, I need to talk
to you, I need to meet you.

00:16:23.760 --> 00:16:26.900
And instead of going back to
Paris, I changed my boarding,

00:16:26.900 --> 00:16:28.650
and I went to New York.

00:16:28.650 --> 00:16:31.470
And I sat with him,
we had breakfast.

00:16:31.470 --> 00:16:36.990
And I said, you don't know me,
but I just discovered a movie

00:16:36.990 --> 00:16:37.950
about your wife.

00:16:37.950 --> 00:16:40.710
And I just--

00:16:40.710 --> 00:16:44.850
[SPEAKING FRENCH],,
as we say in French.

00:16:44.850 --> 00:16:48.090
And I'm so moved,
and I would like

00:16:48.090 --> 00:16:55.365
to do something that she wanted
to do, she has other projects.

00:16:57.990 --> 00:17:01.050
And he told me
about two projects.

00:17:01.050 --> 00:17:06.930
One with about three old
stars, Black stars, you know.

00:17:11.619 --> 00:17:14.880
They took a cruise
in the Caribbean

00:17:14.880 --> 00:17:19.450
and they discovered a new
world, they discovered life,

00:17:19.450 --> 00:17:21.430
they had a good time.

00:17:21.430 --> 00:17:24.420
And then he said,
well, she had also

00:17:24.420 --> 00:17:28.710
a project about to the
first Negro female pilots

00:17:28.710 --> 00:17:30.840
called Bessie Coleman.

00:17:30.840 --> 00:17:36.600
And I said, oh, and he
said, yes, and guess what?

00:17:36.600 --> 00:17:41.670
She went to France, she had to
expatriate herself to France

00:17:41.670 --> 00:17:44.790
for a year in order
to get her license,

00:17:44.790 --> 00:17:52.580
because it was 1918, 1919.

00:17:52.580 --> 00:17:54.720
And I said, you're kidding me?

00:17:54.720 --> 00:17:56.220
In France?

00:17:56.220 --> 00:17:58.320
Yes, and I said, my God.

00:17:58.320 --> 00:18:00.810
And I said, OK, I know why.

00:18:00.810 --> 00:18:07.020
I saw that as a call,
and I said, look,

00:18:07.020 --> 00:18:08.430
I made a promise to you today.

00:18:08.430 --> 00:18:11.820
I'm making the promise
to direct that movie,

00:18:11.820 --> 00:18:14.910
I want to make that
movie for Kathleen.

00:18:14.910 --> 00:18:17.190
And I said, I have to do that.

00:18:17.190 --> 00:18:22.390
And then I went to
Chicago and I met

00:18:22.390 --> 00:18:25.200
Bessie's relative, one
of them, her niece,

00:18:25.200 --> 00:18:28.140
and I found the grave.

00:18:28.140 --> 00:18:32.430
I went there, and I knelt
in front of that grave,

00:18:32.430 --> 00:18:34.410
and I made the same
promise to Bessie.

00:18:34.410 --> 00:18:36.990
And I said look, I made
a promise to Kathleen,

00:18:36.990 --> 00:18:38.440
now I'm here, sister.

00:18:38.440 --> 00:18:42.600
Pray for me wherever
you are, because I'm

00:18:42.600 --> 00:18:45.100
going to hell after Sugar
Cane Alley, [INAUDIBLE]

00:18:45.100 --> 00:18:46.090
didn't want my movie.

00:18:46.090 --> 00:18:48.790
We will go through
hell for your story,

00:18:48.790 --> 00:18:52.410
so I need all the vibrations
that you can send me

00:18:52.410 --> 00:18:53.960
so we can make that movie.

00:18:53.960 --> 00:18:55.890
And you're going
to make that movie,

00:18:55.890 --> 00:18:58.050
and you're still
working on that movie,

00:18:58.050 --> 00:19:01.320
and I'm helping you
make that movie.

00:19:01.320 --> 00:19:03.750
We're making this
movie somehow, but this

00:19:03.750 --> 00:19:11.550
is Euzhan Palcy's Bessie
Coleman, that is a wish project

00:19:11.550 --> 00:19:12.870
Kathleen Collins.

00:19:12.870 --> 00:19:17.500
And this is part of her legacy.

00:19:17.500 --> 00:19:20.070
Julie, Kathleen Collins for you.

00:19:20.070 --> 00:19:21.570
I'm glad that we're
taking some time

00:19:21.570 --> 00:19:24.130
to speak about her
influence on you both.

00:19:24.130 --> 00:19:26.340
Absolutely, Kathleen Collins.

00:19:26.340 --> 00:19:33.060
Well, I think I met
her about 1971 or 1972.

00:19:33.060 --> 00:19:37.740
I was an undergraduate school at
City College in New York City.

00:19:37.740 --> 00:19:41.820
And I was assigned a
summer work study job

00:19:41.820 --> 00:19:43.890
with Chamba Productions.

00:19:43.890 --> 00:19:46.650
Chamba, of course, was
headed by St. Clair

00:19:46.650 --> 00:19:49.020
Bourne, documentary filmmaker.

00:19:49.020 --> 00:19:51.150
And there were five
Chamba brothers--

00:19:51.150 --> 00:19:54.000
Charles Hobson, and
Garrett, all of them.

00:19:54.000 --> 00:19:59.190
And they had just come back from
shooting a film in the South

00:19:59.190 --> 00:20:01.770
called, Let the Church Say Amen.

00:20:01.770 --> 00:20:05.920
And a young woman show up
who was going to edit it.

00:20:05.920 --> 00:20:08.880
And that young woman
was Kathleen Collins.

00:20:08.880 --> 00:20:12.750
And she was fresh out of
the Sorbonne in Paris,

00:20:12.750 --> 00:20:14.070
speaking French--

00:20:14.070 --> 00:20:16.500
a Black woman speaking French--

00:20:16.500 --> 00:20:19.860
and she was a vegetarian.

00:20:19.860 --> 00:20:22.770
So like I always say, like
coming out of the projects,

00:20:22.770 --> 00:20:24.310
I'm like, a vegetarian?

00:20:24.310 --> 00:20:27.750
OK, what is that, exactly?

00:20:27.750 --> 00:20:32.350
So anyway, that summer,
it was like the summer

00:20:32.350 --> 00:20:33.430
of Kathleen Collins.

00:20:33.430 --> 00:20:38.620
She was so friendly and
so competent in her craft.

00:20:38.620 --> 00:20:43.162
She'd be working in the editing
bay, and I'd kind of peek

00:20:43.162 --> 00:20:44.620
in every now and
then, and then she

00:20:44.620 --> 00:20:46.787
said would say, no, come
in, she would say, come in.

00:20:46.787 --> 00:20:51.220
And I would sit with her,
and she would just edit.

00:20:51.220 --> 00:20:54.280
She had a baby, a newborn
baby. sometimes I'd

00:20:54.280 --> 00:20:57.460
sit with her holding the baby.

00:20:57.460 --> 00:21:00.760
It was incredible, but
I just never forgot her.

00:21:00.760 --> 00:21:03.040
And then I after that,
I graduated and went out

00:21:03.040 --> 00:21:04.180
to Los Angeles.

00:21:04.180 --> 00:21:08.740
But she started making films
of her own, feature films--

00:21:08.740 --> 00:21:14.410
The Cruz Brothers and Mrs.
Malloy, Losing Ground,

00:21:14.410 --> 00:21:15.400
all of these films.

00:21:15.400 --> 00:21:20.470
And we stayed in touch, we saw
each other at film festivals

00:21:20.470 --> 00:21:21.410
and things like that.

00:21:21.410 --> 00:21:25.700
And then suddenly
she passed away,

00:21:25.700 --> 00:21:29.320
and I went to her
memorial service.

00:21:29.320 --> 00:21:33.088
All of us were there,
and everyone was a wreck.

00:21:33.088 --> 00:21:37.900
It was shocking, it
was sad, it was tragic,

00:21:37.900 --> 00:21:42.380
because everything
was just cut short,

00:21:42.380 --> 00:21:45.030
just taken away from her.

00:21:45.030 --> 00:21:48.380
And by that time, she
was eating macrobiotics.

00:21:48.380 --> 00:21:51.350
So I was angry, because it
was like, this is not fair,

00:21:51.350 --> 00:21:54.830
she ate well.

00:21:54.830 --> 00:21:59.530
She was so creative, she was
so open, loving, and talented.

00:21:59.530 --> 00:22:02.830
And I just had never met
anyone like her before,

00:22:02.830 --> 00:22:05.720
so she like the
goddess of film for me.

00:22:05.720 --> 00:22:07.025
Yes.

00:22:07.025 --> 00:22:09.280
It's so important, thank
you for sharing that.

00:22:09.280 --> 00:22:11.570
I got emotional as you
were talking about it

00:22:11.570 --> 00:22:14.600
I think it's so important
to talk about her,

00:22:14.600 --> 00:22:23.340
because the Academy, the very
platform that has given us

00:22:23.340 --> 00:22:26.970
this space to talk,
has long excluded

00:22:26.970 --> 00:22:32.130
the voices of women and people
of color in its conversation

00:22:32.130 --> 00:22:34.050
about history.

00:22:34.050 --> 00:22:37.320
I'm so encouraged by
the new museum that's

00:22:37.320 --> 00:22:40.350
being put up by the Academy and
all the great work being done

00:22:40.350 --> 00:22:45.150
by all of the folks there,
a great coalition of people

00:22:45.150 --> 00:22:46.930
to really make
sure that we really

00:22:46.930 --> 00:22:52.170
capture so much of what the
organization had systemically

00:22:52.170 --> 00:22:53.190
ignored.

00:22:53.190 --> 00:22:56.550
And most of us weren't
there at the time

00:22:56.550 --> 00:22:59.610
that those early decisions
were made, but we're here now.

00:22:59.610 --> 00:23:04.530
And so I'm so happy that
she, and both of your work,

00:23:04.530 --> 00:23:06.180
is included in the
museum, and it's

00:23:06.180 --> 00:23:08.940
going to be talked
about as we go forward.

00:23:08.940 --> 00:23:14.460
Speaking of your starts and how
you came up in that early work,

00:23:14.460 --> 00:23:19.620
I just want to move in to talk
a little bit about what many

00:23:19.620 --> 00:23:24.390
would consider the time
when you came on the scene

00:23:24.390 --> 00:23:28.050
and you were able to present
your work to audience.

00:23:28.050 --> 00:23:29.490
And so I'm hoping
that you all can

00:23:29.490 --> 00:23:33.060
talk to me about the
connection and the moments

00:23:33.060 --> 00:23:35.940
when your work has
connected to audience.

00:23:35.940 --> 00:23:38.520
Not necessarily with
the most famous films,

00:23:38.520 --> 00:23:42.120
but the experiences having
maybe not the most robust

00:23:42.120 --> 00:23:44.460
distribution, not
the best marketing,

00:23:44.460 --> 00:23:46.990
but the ways in which,
despite all of that--

00:23:46.990 --> 00:23:51.180
despite lack of consistent
industry recognition--

00:23:51.180 --> 00:23:54.750
you've been able to cultivate
an audience for your work.

00:23:54.750 --> 00:23:59.745
How have you achieved that
over these past decades?

00:24:02.550 --> 00:24:03.990
Persistence of vision.

00:24:03.990 --> 00:24:06.630
[CHUCKLING]

00:24:06.630 --> 00:24:12.570
And not allowing anyone to tell
you what you can and can't do.

00:24:12.570 --> 00:24:18.900
What people don't understand
for the most part, for me,

00:24:18.900 --> 00:24:21.780
I never had dreams of
working in Hollywood.

00:24:21.780 --> 00:24:24.160
I had dreams of
being a storyteller,

00:24:24.160 --> 00:24:28.500
I had dreams of making
films, and experimenting

00:24:28.500 --> 00:24:33.630
with ways of telling a
story, and that I have done.

00:24:33.630 --> 00:24:36.690
I made a little Super
8 film years ago back

00:24:36.690 --> 00:24:40.110
in the '70's that played at
Film Ex, Los Angeles Film

00:24:40.110 --> 00:24:41.580
Exposition.

00:24:41.580 --> 00:24:44.550
And today it's been blown
up to 35 millimeter.

00:24:44.550 --> 00:24:46.362
That cracked me up.

00:24:46.362 --> 00:24:48.180
And it's in distribution.

00:24:48.180 --> 00:24:49.620
It's a little short.

00:24:49.620 --> 00:24:53.590
I did another little short
called Four Women which

00:24:53.590 --> 00:24:57.840
won best film at the Miami
International Film Festival

00:24:57.840 --> 00:25:03.040
maybe in 1976, 1979,
or something like that.

00:25:03.040 --> 00:25:05.810
But it's on the
books as having won,

00:25:05.810 --> 00:25:08.060
and that's in distribution.

00:25:08.060 --> 00:25:16.460
So I enjoy what I do, and yes,
I wish I had the opportunity

00:25:16.460 --> 00:25:18.620
to have more feature
length films,

00:25:18.620 --> 00:25:22.353
but I have about five other
movies made for television.

00:25:22.353 --> 00:25:24.020
But we could get into
that later, I want

00:25:24.020 --> 00:25:28.790
to hear what Euzhan has to say.

00:25:28.790 --> 00:25:31.270
About cultivating
audience over these years,

00:25:31.270 --> 00:25:35.268
Euzhan, when the industry might
not have helped with that,

00:25:35.268 --> 00:25:36.560
might not have encouraged that.

00:25:36.560 --> 00:25:39.600
And yet you persisted.

00:25:39.600 --> 00:25:46.940
Yeah, it was hell,
let's call a cat a cat.

00:25:46.940 --> 00:25:50.590
But persistence is a key thing.

00:25:50.590 --> 00:25:53.620
You have to keep pushing
and pushing the boundaries.

00:25:53.620 --> 00:25:57.750
And when they get you out
the room through the door,

00:25:57.750 --> 00:26:01.920
you come back in the windows.

00:26:01.920 --> 00:26:05.760
That's what I have
been doing for years.

00:26:05.760 --> 00:26:07.440
And you do watch.

00:26:07.440 --> 00:26:08.910
If you come to
feature because you

00:26:08.910 --> 00:26:12.570
don't want your
movies too Black, too

00:26:12.570 --> 00:26:15.030
whatever, [INAUDIBLE] cable.

00:26:15.030 --> 00:26:17.080
Well, you do documentaries--

00:26:17.080 --> 00:26:20.790
I love good documentaries,
because they're

00:26:20.790 --> 00:26:23.880
about people, people's lives,
real things, real lives.

00:26:23.880 --> 00:26:28.320
Or you do series, you
work with a network,

00:26:28.320 --> 00:26:30.320
like I did for Ruby
Bridges for, example.

00:26:30.320 --> 00:26:34.825
I did all of the aftermath of
the Attica Uprising called,

00:26:34.825 --> 00:26:35.970
The Killing Yard.

00:26:35.970 --> 00:26:41.910
And I also did a French
series about the populating

00:26:41.910 --> 00:26:47.410
of the country near Mauritius
island, [SPEAKING FRENCH]..

00:26:47.410 --> 00:26:52.100
At the time it was
a period piece,

00:26:52.100 --> 00:26:57.840
seventeenth century
called Bourbon Island.

00:26:57.840 --> 00:27:02.710
I did a two part series.

00:27:02.710 --> 00:27:05.910
And you both moved into
television which would

00:27:05.910 --> 00:27:08.640
allow you to tell more stories.

00:27:08.640 --> 00:27:11.670
Julie has a
beautiful filmography

00:27:11.670 --> 00:27:18.120
with telefilms, your wonderful
work on Rosa Parks with Angela

00:27:18.120 --> 00:27:19.120
Bassett.

00:27:19.120 --> 00:27:21.630
So much of the work that
you've both done in television.

00:27:21.630 --> 00:27:24.390
The reason why I asked the
question about consistency

00:27:24.390 --> 00:27:27.780
and how you continue on to the
industry providing audience

00:27:27.780 --> 00:27:30.390
for you, is because
I've learned so much

00:27:30.390 --> 00:27:31.890
from that about the idea of.

00:27:31.890 --> 00:27:35.730
Mastery, I use
that word, Julie--

00:27:35.730 --> 00:27:39.420
when I hear the word mastery, I
think of Kerry James Marshall.

00:27:39.420 --> 00:27:44.910
Kerry James Marshall, just
one of the finest artists

00:27:44.910 --> 00:27:49.230
on the planet whose
work, he can just

00:27:49.230 --> 00:27:51.870
decide he wants to throw
some paint on a canvas

00:27:51.870 --> 00:27:56.090
and it's going to sell
for a cool $20 mil.

00:27:56.090 --> 00:27:58.620
And it was the
production designer

00:27:58.620 --> 00:28:01.150
on Daughters of the Dust.

00:28:01.150 --> 00:28:03.980
But the idea of
mastery, the idea

00:28:03.980 --> 00:28:09.370
of who gets to choose
who is a master.

00:28:09.370 --> 00:28:13.290
We are sitting here on a
platform from the Academy,

00:28:13.290 --> 00:28:18.910
and you all have had
great achievement.

00:28:18.910 --> 00:28:20.970
But the idea of
mastery being connected

00:28:20.970 --> 00:28:24.760
to audience and your
work, not the achievement,

00:28:24.760 --> 00:28:27.870
is something that I've
learned from both of you,

00:28:27.870 --> 00:28:32.370
because you are masters, you
are auteurs of great note.

00:28:32.370 --> 00:28:36.330
And yet the industry has
never fully acknowledged that,

00:28:36.330 --> 00:28:38.640
and welcomed you, and
did not help you along,

00:28:38.640 --> 00:28:40.840
and yet you achieved it.

00:28:40.840 --> 00:28:43.620
So I want to ask you about
that idea of looking back, you

00:28:43.620 --> 00:28:46.580
see contemporaries
of yours who are

00:28:46.580 --> 00:28:50.760
your white male counterparts
who were immediately considered

00:28:50.760 --> 00:28:53.730
masters of their field,
and the industry rose up

00:28:53.730 --> 00:28:56.230
to support them.

00:28:56.230 --> 00:28:58.320
How do you reconcile
that day to day

00:28:58.320 --> 00:29:02.680
and be able to stay
healthy in your own work?

00:29:02.680 --> 00:29:06.520
Well, it's interesting, because
I was in AFI the same time

00:29:06.520 --> 00:29:14.290
as Amy Heckerling, Mimi Leder,
Marshall Herskovitz, Ed Zwick,

00:29:14.290 --> 00:29:19.348
all these people who have a
long list of feature films

00:29:19.348 --> 00:29:20.515
that they were able to make.

00:29:23.110 --> 00:29:26.650
And I look at that
sometimes, and I say,

00:29:26.650 --> 00:29:30.070
I wonder where I would have
been or what I could have done.

00:29:30.070 --> 00:29:34.750
But then on the other side of
that is, I would not perhaps

00:29:34.750 --> 00:29:38.140
have been able to say, I
want Kerry James Marshall

00:29:38.140 --> 00:29:43.330
to production design my film if
I had been working in industry.

00:29:43.330 --> 00:29:45.730
Daughters of the Dust was
a very independent film.

00:29:45.730 --> 00:29:49.780
I would not have been able
to say I want Arthur J for AJ

00:29:49.780 --> 00:29:54.960
to be the DP on my
film, which he was,

00:29:54.960 --> 00:29:59.940
and he won award for
best cinematography

00:29:59.940 --> 00:30:04.650
at Sundance for that film.

00:30:04.650 --> 00:30:11.760
So in many ways working
with masters of art--

00:30:11.760 --> 00:30:16.050
being able to recognize,
and pull them in, and work

00:30:16.050 --> 00:30:19.980
with them was a wonderful
thing that added

00:30:19.980 --> 00:30:22.590
to whatever I was trying to do.

00:30:22.590 --> 00:30:25.200
You know the old saying, you
always want to be in a room

00:30:25.200 --> 00:30:27.607
with people who are
smarter than you.

00:30:27.607 --> 00:30:29.190
Well, I've always
want to be in a room

00:30:29.190 --> 00:30:32.585
with people who have a
mastery of the craft greater

00:30:32.585 --> 00:30:35.970
than my own so I can learn too.

00:30:35.970 --> 00:30:39.600
And it is just a joy to
work with people like that.

00:30:39.600 --> 00:30:42.640
And I've done that all along.

00:30:42.640 --> 00:30:44.970
I've also done a
lot of museum work.

00:30:44.970 --> 00:30:48.630
Films for museums,
commercials, car commercials,

00:30:48.630 --> 00:30:49.650
and all those things.

00:30:49.650 --> 00:30:52.180
And on each production, no
matter how large or small,

00:30:52.180 --> 00:30:54.660
I learn something,
and it's wonderful.

00:30:54.660 --> 00:30:57.690
So you've been able to
harmonize that for yourself

00:30:57.690 --> 00:31:02.950
and see the positives in
what you were able to do.

00:31:02.950 --> 00:31:03.450
Absolutely.

00:31:03.450 --> 00:31:05.460
I think it's
important, because when

00:31:05.460 --> 00:31:09.600
I hear people talk about you
and Euzhan, what I hate is the,

00:31:09.600 --> 00:31:14.490
oh, if they only,
you know, poor--

00:31:14.490 --> 00:31:17.010
these women are
dynamic and vibrant.

00:31:17.010 --> 00:31:19.320
Euzhan, what is your thought
about that whole idea

00:31:19.320 --> 00:31:23.670
of mastery and who gets to
be the masters of cinema

00:31:23.670 --> 00:31:26.650
and how you fit into that?

00:31:26.650 --> 00:31:34.980
Well, I would like to say that
when I did Sugar Cane Alley,

00:31:34.980 --> 00:31:39.540
I didn't have a production
company, but I had some money.

00:31:39.540 --> 00:31:42.240
I raised some money,
and French government

00:31:42.240 --> 00:31:45.510
helped to get me a grant.

00:31:45.510 --> 00:31:47.640
I did a job--

00:31:47.640 --> 00:31:49.440
the producer's job-- as well.

00:31:49.440 --> 00:31:53.610
I wrote the screenplay,
and I directed the film,

00:31:53.610 --> 00:31:56.400
and also, I acted
as a producer, but I

00:31:56.400 --> 00:31:59.610
didn't get any credit for that,
because I didn't even care,

00:31:59.610 --> 00:32:02.040
because I just wanted
to make that movie.

00:32:02.040 --> 00:32:05.790
And to picture the
people that helped me,

00:32:05.790 --> 00:32:07.920
because that was my first film.

00:32:07.920 --> 00:32:13.050
And I went to one of the
best film school in Paris,

00:32:13.050 --> 00:32:13.710
but still.

00:32:13.710 --> 00:32:22.570
And I was the first Black woman
coming out of that school,

00:32:22.570 --> 00:32:25.890
Louis Lumiére acting school.

00:32:25.890 --> 00:32:29.030
François Truffaut
is one, the French,

00:32:29.030 --> 00:32:34.680
that very famous
French director.

00:32:34.680 --> 00:32:42.100
And he's the one who
advised me about the crew.

00:32:42.100 --> 00:32:48.990
He suggested some masters
in different categories.

00:32:48.990 --> 00:32:57.360
And that's how I was able to
build my first creative crew,

00:32:57.360 --> 00:33:00.790
bring people together,
to do my first thing.

00:33:00.790 --> 00:33:04.980
After that, I didn't
want to go to Hollywood

00:33:04.980 --> 00:33:09.865
to work with Hollywood,
because Hollywood attracted me.

00:33:09.865 --> 00:33:10.740
I never thought that.

00:33:10.740 --> 00:33:13.170
I would fit in, because
it's a totally different way

00:33:13.170 --> 00:33:15.140
of working.

00:33:15.140 --> 00:33:19.300
If you compare Hollywood and
Europe, it's totally different.

00:33:19.300 --> 00:33:25.760
And in fact, [INAUDIBLE],, it's a
long story, but after Sundance,

00:33:25.760 --> 00:33:29.730
the five letters that Warner
Brothers sent me to ask me

00:33:29.730 --> 00:33:32.340
to come, because he loved--

00:33:32.340 --> 00:33:34.690
We have to go back a step,
because Robert Redford,

00:33:34.690 --> 00:33:38.070
you were in the
first Sundance Labs,

00:33:38.070 --> 00:33:40.860
Robert Redford
invited you up when

00:33:40.860 --> 00:33:42.480
no one knew what Sundance was.

00:33:42.480 --> 00:33:44.340
He's in the woods, where is he?

00:33:44.340 --> 00:33:45.180
What is he doing?

00:33:45.180 --> 00:33:46.920
OK, I'll go.

00:33:46.920 --> 00:33:51.090
And he was also a part of the
connection with you to Sony,

00:33:51.090 --> 00:33:51.590
right?

00:33:51.590 --> 00:33:53.030
For A Dry White Season?

00:33:53.030 --> 00:33:56.070
A Dry White Season, yes,
with Warner Brothers.

00:33:56.070 --> 00:33:58.500
Yeah, Warner Brothers, OK.

00:33:58.500 --> 00:34:01.200
And because what
he did, in fact,

00:34:01.200 --> 00:34:04.290
when you got to Sundance Lab,
you need to have a project,

00:34:04.290 --> 00:34:06.940
and I came with a book, A Dry
White Season written by André

00:34:06.940 --> 00:34:07.440
Brink.

00:34:10.710 --> 00:34:13.530
So he was very
attentive to my work,

00:34:13.530 --> 00:34:17.880
and we had many conversations
sitting on the grass.

00:34:17.880 --> 00:34:21.008
And one day he said, OK,
so now you are going back

00:34:21.008 --> 00:34:22.300
home, what are you going to do?

00:34:22.300 --> 00:34:24.360
I said, well, I
don't know, yeah, I

00:34:24.360 --> 00:34:25.620
think I will go back home.

00:34:25.620 --> 00:34:27.210
And I showed him
the five letters

00:34:27.210 --> 00:34:29.639
that Lucy Fisher
from Warner Brothers

00:34:29.639 --> 00:34:34.139
wrote me to ask me insistently,
come, and let's work together,

00:34:34.139 --> 00:34:37.679
we would like to work with
you, we adore Sugar Cane Alley.

00:34:37.679 --> 00:34:40.350
And he said, OK, you should
go, you should try it.

00:34:40.350 --> 00:34:41.159
You should try it.

00:34:41.159 --> 00:34:43.350
And I said, no, I
don't want my life

00:34:43.350 --> 00:34:48.120
to be miserable, because I
don't think I will would fit in,

00:34:48.120 --> 00:34:51.210
because I know what I want,
I know how I want to do this,

00:34:51.210 --> 00:34:54.870
and to be able to decide for
my stories and everything.

00:34:54.870 --> 00:34:57.240
And that's not always
how Hollywood works.

00:34:57.240 --> 00:34:59.760
And he said, no, I think
that you should go.

00:34:59.760 --> 00:35:01.710
You are a strong woman.

00:35:01.710 --> 00:35:05.490
Go and just discuss
with them, and try it.

00:35:05.490 --> 00:35:08.340
And then he called Lucy Fisher,
they put me on the plane,

00:35:08.340 --> 00:35:12.290
and that's how I
arrived in Hollywood.

00:35:12.290 --> 00:35:14.080
And I came with that project.

00:35:14.080 --> 00:35:18.900
And it was not easy,
Lucy was very happy,

00:35:18.900 --> 00:35:21.030
she offered me a lot of movies.

00:35:21.030 --> 00:35:23.460
But I said no, no, no, no.

00:35:23.460 --> 00:35:24.840
She even offered me--

00:35:28.740 --> 00:35:32.220
sorry, I forget the title.

00:35:32.220 --> 00:35:32.910
But that's OK.

00:35:32.910 --> 00:35:34.985
[CHUCKLING]

00:35:35.960 --> 00:35:39.750
But cut to, you end up
directing Marlon Brando

00:35:39.750 --> 00:35:45.630
and being the first Black
director to direct an actor

00:35:45.630 --> 00:35:48.280
to an Oscar nomination.

00:35:48.280 --> 00:35:50.700
And you also won
the César, right?

00:35:50.700 --> 00:35:54.270
Was it the César for A Dry White
Season or for Sugar Cane Alley?

00:35:54.270 --> 00:36:02.240
Oh, for Sugar Cane Alley, yes,
I am the first female director

00:36:02.240 --> 00:36:07.410
who got César, the French César,
you know the French Oscar,

00:36:07.410 --> 00:36:11.270
I was once with
Americans for a movie.

00:36:11.270 --> 00:36:11.770
Yes.

00:36:11.770 --> 00:36:14.940
And so this idea
of mastery, do you

00:36:14.940 --> 00:36:19.140
feel like a master of
cinema, let me ask you that?

00:36:19.140 --> 00:36:20.170
A master?

00:36:20.170 --> 00:36:21.240
A master.

00:36:21.240 --> 00:36:22.810
Am I saying the
right word in French?

00:36:22.810 --> 00:36:24.524
Like a master.

00:36:27.830 --> 00:36:33.690
You know what, I always
saw myself like a shoemaker

00:36:33.690 --> 00:36:34.580
on a mission.

00:36:34.580 --> 00:36:35.970
[CHUCKLING]

00:36:35.970 --> 00:36:37.920
And fighting to do--

00:36:37.920 --> 00:36:41.800
I want to make films, I want--

00:36:41.800 --> 00:36:45.530
I used to say that
with my camera

00:36:45.530 --> 00:36:49.530
that I call my
miraculous weapon,

00:36:49.530 --> 00:36:58.020
I'm trying to heal the
wounds created by history.

00:36:58.020 --> 00:37:02.150
I'm trying to reveal things
that had been hidden,

00:37:02.150 --> 00:37:08.580
or destroyed, or stolen.

00:37:08.580 --> 00:37:11.190
And I'm trying to
talk about this

00:37:11.190 --> 00:37:13.362
and to bring people together.

00:37:13.362 --> 00:37:14.570
That's what I'm trying to do.

00:37:14.570 --> 00:37:16.050
That's I want to do that with--

00:37:16.050 --> 00:37:21.780
I'm doing it, and I try to
keep doing it with respect,

00:37:21.780 --> 00:37:28.480
with love, with
passion, and a smile.

00:37:28.480 --> 00:37:33.950
[INAUDIBLE] smile and smile.

00:37:33.950 --> 00:37:35.340
That's right.

00:37:35.340 --> 00:37:37.980
Well, I consider
you both masters.

00:37:37.980 --> 00:37:41.400
And just as we start to wrap up,
I want to talk about the things

00:37:41.400 --> 00:37:43.295
that you have ahead,
because I think

00:37:43.295 --> 00:37:44.670
it's really
important that people

00:37:44.670 --> 00:37:51.070
know that you are filmmakers,
and you are making work.

00:37:51.070 --> 00:37:53.160
This is not a time
capsule, we're

00:37:53.160 --> 00:37:56.490
not opening up the
old vaults to say,

00:37:56.490 --> 00:37:58.560
look at what they
did, we want to say,

00:37:58.560 --> 00:38:00.150
look at what they're doing.

00:38:00.150 --> 00:38:02.790
So I just want to invite
you to talk a little bit--

00:38:02.790 --> 00:38:05.760
I'll start with Euzhan
and end with Julie--

00:38:05.760 --> 00:38:08.610
about your projects that you're
working on now and things

00:38:08.610 --> 00:38:11.800
that you're excited about.

00:38:11.800 --> 00:38:18.330
Well, actually another
project that I had

00:38:18.330 --> 00:38:21.130
and that were dear to
my heart and that he

00:38:21.130 --> 00:38:26.050
rejected for the same
reason that we talk about.

00:38:26.050 --> 00:38:34.190
Because you would think that you
are not worthy- to open a movie

00:38:34.190 --> 00:38:42.230
and win an audience in the
series or to be sold to foreign

00:38:42.230 --> 00:38:45.500
what was a [INAUDIBLE] mistake.

00:38:45.500 --> 00:38:48.470
So now I have been revisiting
all these projects,

00:38:48.470 --> 00:38:51.470
because this is the right time.

00:38:51.470 --> 00:38:54.950
I truly believe I was very
patient, I did other stuff,

00:38:54.950 --> 00:38:57.500
I did a lot of
writing, and I have

00:38:57.500 --> 00:39:00.680
a few projects on the table.

00:39:00.680 --> 00:39:05.830
Like Bessie Coleman, of course,
Bessie Coleman, and the story

00:39:05.830 --> 00:39:10.610
is set in the States
and in France.

00:39:10.610 --> 00:39:13.490
And I also have comedies.

00:39:13.490 --> 00:39:16.680
I have one that
is a multi-racial

00:39:16.680 --> 00:39:20.360
French and American
comedy set in Hollywood,

00:39:20.360 --> 00:39:26.090
a very great restaurant.

00:39:26.090 --> 00:39:33.430
And I have also a true
story called Blackville.

00:39:33.430 --> 00:39:40.940
The story takes place in 1864,
Baltimore, Paris, and Germany.

00:39:40.940 --> 00:39:44.740
And it is a biracial
love story, and there

00:39:44.740 --> 00:39:49.300
is everything in it-- action,
politics, it's a thriller.

00:39:49.300 --> 00:39:52.865
It's a big piece, fantastic.

00:39:52.865 --> 00:39:55.000
[INAUDIBLE]

00:39:55.000 --> 00:39:59.580
Wow, three projects you
have in your back pocket.

00:39:59.580 --> 00:40:00.450
Busy.

00:40:00.450 --> 00:40:01.870
Miss Dash?

00:40:01.870 --> 00:40:03.910
You're working on
99 things as well?

00:40:03.910 --> 00:40:05.800
You're teaching at Spelman.

00:40:05.800 --> 00:40:09.340
I'm teaching at Spelman,
I have a documentary

00:40:09.340 --> 00:40:12.670
in progress called Travel
Notes of a Geechee Girl.

00:40:12.670 --> 00:40:15.940
And it's about Vertamae
Smart-Grosvenor, who

00:40:15.940 --> 00:40:20.070
was a culinary anthropologist.

00:40:20.070 --> 00:40:23.380
She was a close friend, she
passed away two years ago,

00:40:23.380 --> 00:40:26.110
and she would advise me
on Daughters of the Dust,

00:40:26.110 --> 00:40:27.790
she was from the region too.

00:40:27.790 --> 00:40:32.260
I'm also working on
a film for Lionsgate.

00:40:32.260 --> 00:40:36.770
We're developing the
autobiography of Angela Davis.

00:40:36.770 --> 00:40:37.440
Yay!

00:40:37.440 --> 00:40:38.820
Dramatic feature.

00:40:38.820 --> 00:40:42.030
We've been working
on that for a while.

00:40:42.030 --> 00:40:45.720
But I have another project,
and it's a limited series,

00:40:45.720 --> 00:40:49.780
and it's called Eleanor
Roosevelt's Battalion,

00:40:49.780 --> 00:40:54.370
and it's about the 850
African-American women who

00:40:54.370 --> 00:40:58.030
served overseas
during World War II,

00:40:58.030 --> 00:41:02.380
and they're also known as
the 6888 Postal Battalion.

00:41:02.380 --> 00:41:05.350
So that is a project
that's really dear to me,

00:41:05.350 --> 00:41:10.330
and hopefully we could
get that going this year.

00:41:10.330 --> 00:41:13.210
Yes, very busy.

00:41:13.210 --> 00:41:16.030
And let me ask you, though all
these projects sound amazing,

00:41:16.030 --> 00:41:17.740
let me ask you one
other question.

00:41:17.740 --> 00:41:20.140
I just want to ask
a craft question,

00:41:20.140 --> 00:41:23.280
because, like I said, so often
we're asked about identity,

00:41:23.280 --> 00:41:25.780
we're asked about our histories,
our culture, our community,

00:41:25.780 --> 00:41:27.300
all of which we love.

00:41:27.300 --> 00:41:28.960
But as women
filmmakers, especially

00:41:28.960 --> 00:41:34.270
as Black women filmmakers, we're
so rarely asked about craft.

00:41:34.270 --> 00:41:37.690
So I want to do a speed round.

00:41:37.690 --> 00:41:41.100
Euzhan, speed round.

00:41:41.100 --> 00:41:43.090
Miss Dash, speed round.

00:41:43.090 --> 00:41:45.755
I'm going to ask the
question, and then we're

00:41:45.755 --> 00:41:47.380
going to go back to
back on the answer.

00:41:47.380 --> 00:41:49.240
I'm going to try
to get in three.

00:41:49.240 --> 00:41:50.050
Ready?

00:41:50.050 --> 00:41:50.903
OK.

00:41:50.903 --> 00:41:53.365
[GIGGLING]

00:41:54.670 --> 00:42:01.240
OK, when you are at on
set and you are rolling,

00:42:01.240 --> 00:42:07.120
do you like to be in video
village at your monitors?

00:42:07.120 --> 00:42:11.590
Or do you like to be watching
the action with your eye?

00:42:11.590 --> 00:42:13.840
Do you prefer
monitors or your eye?

00:42:13.840 --> 00:42:16.160
Euzhan?

00:42:16.160 --> 00:42:17.550
The monitors.

00:42:17.550 --> 00:42:19.010
Monitors.

00:42:19.010 --> 00:42:20.090
Julie?

00:42:20.090 --> 00:42:21.350
Monitors.

00:42:21.350 --> 00:42:26.260
I never had monitors before,
now I do, so monitors.

00:42:26.260 --> 00:42:28.700
You're like, I've got monitors,
I'm not letting them go.

00:42:28.700 --> 00:42:30.400
I couldn't afford
them before, go ahead.

00:42:30.400 --> 00:42:32.180
OK, all right.

00:42:32.180 --> 00:42:33.710
Rehearsal.

00:42:33.710 --> 00:42:37.080
OK, rehearsal techniques--
what is your trick?

00:42:37.080 --> 00:42:42.650
You have an actor on set, stuck,
can't get it, not getting it,

00:42:42.650 --> 00:42:46.190
time is ticking, you
gotta do something.

00:42:46.190 --> 00:42:47.510
What do you pull out?

00:42:47.510 --> 00:42:50.720
What's that the Julie Dash
trick to get it going?

00:42:50.720 --> 00:42:51.740
Julie?

00:42:51.740 --> 00:42:56.450
I ask them to speak the role
of the person acting against,

00:42:56.450 --> 00:42:58.840
so I flip it.

00:42:58.840 --> 00:43:00.200
And it frees them up?

00:43:00.200 --> 00:43:02.000
Yeah, it frees them
up, because then they

00:43:02.000 --> 00:43:04.130
can understand the
other person's position,

00:43:04.130 --> 00:43:06.290
and then they could go
back to theirs, and say oh.

00:43:06.290 --> 00:43:08.930
Ooh, I can use that one.

00:43:08.930 --> 00:43:09.470
[GIGGLING]

00:43:09.470 --> 00:43:11.400
Euzhan?

00:43:11.400 --> 00:43:14.080
Well, to answer for that
question I'll take an example.

00:43:14.080 --> 00:43:17.570
In A Dry White Season, there
is a very tough, very emotional

00:43:17.570 --> 00:43:20.570
scene when Stanley,
who is a Black South

00:43:20.570 --> 00:43:24.380
Africans, a Black cab
driver, he gave it

00:43:24.380 --> 00:43:28.570
to the white guys,
the white lead,

00:43:28.570 --> 00:43:32.370
his colleague's
house at Christmas.

00:43:32.370 --> 00:43:37.070
And he came, and
in fact, he came

00:43:37.070 --> 00:43:39.470
to deliver some
kind of bad news,

00:43:39.470 --> 00:43:42.140
and he's supposed to be crying.

00:43:42.140 --> 00:43:44.700
He would be in tears,
he would be in tears.

00:43:44.700 --> 00:43:47.840
He couldn't cry, Zakes
Mokae, he couldn't cry.

00:43:47.840 --> 00:43:51.110
We tried everything,
and time was running.

00:43:51.110 --> 00:43:54.680
And I said, OK, we
stop everything,

00:43:54.680 --> 00:43:57.730
and I said, stay put,
stay tuned, guys.

00:43:57.730 --> 00:43:59.930
And I walked outside
with him, and I said,

00:43:59.930 --> 00:44:01.550
Stanley, what is going on?

00:44:01.550 --> 00:44:02.450
Talk to me fast.

00:44:02.450 --> 00:44:06.150
He said, I don't
know, I can't cry.

00:44:06.150 --> 00:44:09.097
And I said, OK, you
are South African,

00:44:09.097 --> 00:44:11.430
I know that they killed your
brother, they tortured him,

00:44:11.430 --> 00:44:14.157
they hang him, and they said
that he committed suicide.

00:44:14.157 --> 00:44:15.740
They did the same
thing with your dad.

00:44:15.740 --> 00:44:18.410
Do you remember when
you got the news?

00:44:18.410 --> 00:44:20.540
How you felt about this?

00:44:20.540 --> 00:44:21.630
He said, yes.

00:44:21.630 --> 00:44:25.040
And I said, I want you to go
back to room, have the scene,

00:44:25.040 --> 00:44:27.130
and then when you
get to the floor,

00:44:27.130 --> 00:44:30.110
then you see your
brother, you see your dad,

00:44:30.110 --> 00:44:33.360
and you remember that
emotion, give it to me.

00:44:33.360 --> 00:44:35.010
And that's exactly what he did.

00:44:35.010 --> 00:44:37.980
And he was crying like crazy.

00:44:37.980 --> 00:44:38.760
That's it.

00:44:38.760 --> 00:44:41.990
You tapped into the
personal, the backstory.

00:44:41.990 --> 00:44:42.650
Got it.

00:44:42.650 --> 00:44:44.510
You make them remember.

00:44:44.510 --> 00:44:47.560
Third and last
question about craft--

00:44:47.560 --> 00:44:49.840
why doesn't anyone
talk about post?

00:44:49.840 --> 00:44:53.620
It's part of my favorite part
of the process, love it so much.

00:44:53.620 --> 00:44:55.900
We have all different
parts of post.

00:44:55.900 --> 00:44:57.730
As a Black woman
filmmaker I'm so

00:44:57.730 --> 00:45:00.100
rarely asked about my
process, and no one

00:45:00.100 --> 00:45:02.380
asks anybody about post.

00:45:02.380 --> 00:45:05.500
So we know there's so many
different parts of post.

00:45:05.500 --> 00:45:07.450
What is your favorite
part of post?

00:45:07.450 --> 00:45:08.560
Editorial?

00:45:08.560 --> 00:45:09.370
Music?

00:45:09.370 --> 00:45:10.360
Color?

00:45:10.360 --> 00:45:11.155
VFX?

00:45:11.155 --> 00:45:12.030
What's your favorite?

00:45:12.030 --> 00:45:13.800
Euzhan?

00:45:13.800 --> 00:45:17.130
Well, I love everything,
but my favorite?

00:45:17.130 --> 00:45:22.490
I would say the
editing, but also

00:45:22.490 --> 00:45:25.200
when we put all the
little things around.

00:45:28.720 --> 00:45:33.620
And to create an atmosphere,
[INAUDIBLE] to things.

00:45:33.620 --> 00:45:38.630
Because I believe that the
directing, mise-en-scène,

00:45:38.630 --> 00:45:41.230
it goes that far.

00:45:41.230 --> 00:45:42.770
Yes.

00:45:42.770 --> 00:45:46.850
And after you do that, that's
a moment, one of the moments

00:45:46.850 --> 00:45:47.900
that I love the most.

00:45:47.900 --> 00:45:51.450
I love the directing a
lot, and I love the post.

00:45:51.450 --> 00:45:54.020
Two things I adore.

00:45:54.020 --> 00:45:57.564
Yes, what is your favorite
part of post, Miss Dash?

00:45:57.564 --> 00:46:01.760
Editing, because that's
where the film is made.

00:46:01.760 --> 00:46:05.240
Editing is a continuation
of directing.

00:46:05.240 --> 00:46:05.810
Yes.

00:46:05.810 --> 00:46:09.410
You start carving out scenes
to make them even better.

00:46:09.410 --> 00:46:12.200
Even better, even better.

00:46:12.200 --> 00:46:13.790
Anything I haven't
asked you that you

00:46:13.790 --> 00:46:14.975
want to make sure we say?

00:46:17.950 --> 00:46:19.480
Anything?

00:46:19.480 --> 00:46:24.510
Well, I would like
to address, Julie,

00:46:24.510 --> 00:46:27.210
if you feel like, a message
to the young generation,

00:46:27.210 --> 00:46:34.970
because people consider us
as trailblazers, Julie and I.

00:46:34.970 --> 00:46:41.290
But I feel like it
hurts me to realize

00:46:41.290 --> 00:46:44.950
that a young generation, even
if we paved the way for many,

00:46:44.950 --> 00:46:47.310
but that's not the problem.

00:46:47.310 --> 00:46:50.230
But they don't know
about our work,

00:46:50.230 --> 00:46:56.850
because nobody teaches
them about Julie's work

00:46:56.850 --> 00:46:58.870
and about my work.

00:46:58.870 --> 00:47:01.690
They don't even know we exist.

00:47:01.690 --> 00:47:03.220
And I think that
there is something

00:47:03.220 --> 00:47:09.700
that we as the Academy
needs to do about it make

00:47:09.700 --> 00:47:14.230
them know that we existed, we
were there, we did the work,

00:47:14.230 --> 00:47:18.310
we carry the fight,
and the fight is not

00:47:18.310 --> 00:47:21.440
over to bring Black folks.

00:47:21.440 --> 00:47:26.380
Because we have been fighting
for decades to bring diversity

00:47:26.380 --> 00:47:27.440
on the screen.

00:47:27.440 --> 00:47:29.410
That's what we have been
doing, right, Julie?

00:47:29.410 --> 00:47:30.470
Yes, absolutely.

00:47:30.470 --> 00:47:33.010
And I think the most
disheartening thing

00:47:33.010 --> 00:47:37.960
is when my agent is told that
I don't have enough experience.

00:47:37.960 --> 00:47:38.810
[GASP]

00:47:38.810 --> 00:47:40.150
I know.

00:47:40.150 --> 00:47:41.580
Oh my god.

00:47:41.580 --> 00:47:43.150
How old was your agent?

00:47:43.150 --> 00:47:45.898
[LAUGHING]

00:47:46.850 --> 00:47:49.460
I hope your agent gives
them a mouth full.

00:47:49.460 --> 00:47:51.470
Well, I hope so.

00:47:51.470 --> 00:47:53.470
He's really great.

00:47:53.470 --> 00:47:59.960
But that's what I've been told
by very episodic producers

00:47:59.960 --> 00:48:02.730
and show runners.

00:48:02.730 --> 00:48:08.830
You see, that's why we need
to be at the table as decision

00:48:08.830 --> 00:48:10.990
makers in all departments--

00:48:10.990 --> 00:48:19.950
screenplay, writing,
production, distribution,

00:48:19.950 --> 00:48:24.070
behind the camera, in front
of the camera, everywhere.

00:48:24.070 --> 00:48:26.080
Everybody, all the diversity--

00:48:26.080 --> 00:48:31.760
Native American, Black,
African-American, Latino,

00:48:31.760 --> 00:48:33.550
Asian.

00:48:33.550 --> 00:48:34.870
Yes.

00:48:34.870 --> 00:48:35.890
You're right.

00:48:35.890 --> 00:48:37.450
I want to end this by--

00:48:42.370 --> 00:48:44.470
first of all, thank
you so much for this.

00:48:44.470 --> 00:48:51.070
If you can direct someone who
has never seen any of your work

00:48:51.070 --> 00:48:55.630
to one scene in
any of your films--

00:48:55.630 --> 00:48:58.650
this is a hard one.

00:48:58.650 --> 00:49:01.820
Y'all have made a lot
of films, a lot of TV,

00:49:01.820 --> 00:49:03.680
a lot of beautiful stories.

00:49:03.680 --> 00:49:06.790
But if there's one scene
that you can say, watch this,

00:49:06.790 --> 00:49:09.160
and you will understand
a bit more about me.

00:49:09.160 --> 00:49:12.570
This scene will introduce
you to me and my work.

00:49:12.570 --> 00:49:13.540
What would that be?

00:49:16.220 --> 00:49:18.650
Actually, for me, it would
be a little unknown film

00:49:18.650 --> 00:49:23.720
I did with the Urban Bush
Women called Praise House.

00:49:23.720 --> 00:49:28.860
It's about a young
artist who doesn't speak,

00:49:28.860 --> 00:49:32.910
her grandmother speaks for her.

00:49:32.910 --> 00:49:34.910
And what's the scene?

00:49:34.910 --> 00:49:39.500
The scene of her
drawing in a huge Bible,

00:49:39.500 --> 00:49:43.280
and her grandmother's
narrating who she is,

00:49:43.280 --> 00:49:47.510
and what she does, and doesn't
understand why she continues on

00:49:47.510 --> 00:49:49.640
with her art.

00:49:49.640 --> 00:49:51.460
It's really quirky.

00:49:51.460 --> 00:49:53.410
OK, all right.

00:49:53.410 --> 00:49:54.480
I like that.

00:49:54.480 --> 00:49:56.110
Women are leaping
around the place

00:49:56.110 --> 00:49:59.290
and doing backflips,
and dancing, and stuff.

00:49:59.290 --> 00:50:01.900
Yeah, that's kind of me.

00:50:01.900 --> 00:50:03.020
That's you, OK.

00:50:03.020 --> 00:50:05.890
I'm going to try to find that.

00:50:05.890 --> 00:50:08.650
And for you, Miss Palcy?

00:50:08.650 --> 00:50:14.680
Well, I guess the scene in
Sugar Cane Alley, my first film.

00:50:14.680 --> 00:50:18.490
When the grandmother
decided to got

00:50:18.490 --> 00:50:23.860
to the capital 25 or
30 kilometers away

00:50:23.860 --> 00:50:28.720
from our home, her town, just
to establish herself living

00:50:28.720 --> 00:50:34.230
in a box that they used to
carry American cow to bring

00:50:34.230 --> 00:50:36.100
to the country, to Martinique.

00:50:36.100 --> 00:50:39.400
And then people will
take those boxes

00:50:39.400 --> 00:50:44.200
and transform that into a home
for them, the poor people.

00:50:44.200 --> 00:50:48.790
And she will do that just to
take her son to the school,

00:50:48.790 --> 00:50:54.130
because he had a grant,
and he had to go to school.

00:50:54.130 --> 00:51:00.040
And how, in that scene,
in the colonial era,

00:51:00.040 --> 00:51:05.910
and how the people will
deal with her and the child.

00:51:05.910 --> 00:51:11.140
And it's a very moving scene,
heartbreaking, but so strong,

00:51:11.140 --> 00:51:13.330
because that old woman
said, OK, they are

00:51:13.330 --> 00:51:14.950
doing that to discourage me.

00:51:14.950 --> 00:51:18.990
But you are going
to go to school.

00:51:18.990 --> 00:51:22.510
Yes, it's about education,
because all my movies

00:51:22.510 --> 00:51:29.190
are about love, about
education, about

00:51:29.190 --> 00:51:36.970
intergenerational exchange
and about love too.

00:51:36.970 --> 00:51:38.210
About love.

00:51:38.210 --> 00:51:40.960
Well, I love you both.

00:51:40.960 --> 00:51:44.890
And I just want to take this
opportunity to thank you for--

00:51:44.890 --> 00:51:49.220
I'm not going to start crying--
being so loving and kind to me

00:51:49.220 --> 00:51:51.880
at every time that I've
crossed paths with you.

00:51:51.880 --> 00:51:55.470
When I first showed show
my film at Sundance,

00:51:55.470 --> 00:52:01.150
in the very first screening
of Middle of Nowhere,

00:52:01.150 --> 00:52:04.690
a woman stood up in the
audience and asked the question

00:52:04.690 --> 00:52:08.425
in a Q&amp;A. But it wasn't a
question, it was Julie Dash,

00:52:08.425 --> 00:52:11.620
and she was saying
she was proud of me.

00:52:11.620 --> 00:52:15.760
And Euzhan, you have, whenever
you come to the States,

00:52:15.760 --> 00:52:20.890
you call me, you make sure that
we connect and break bread.

00:52:20.890 --> 00:52:24.760
You've allowed me to share
you with other Black women

00:52:24.760 --> 00:52:28.180
directors so that
they can meet you.

00:52:28.180 --> 00:52:31.160
You both are huge
parts of my journey,

00:52:31.160 --> 00:52:34.580
and I just want everyone
to know about you--

00:52:34.580 --> 00:52:36.940
what you've done,
what you're doing,

00:52:36.940 --> 00:52:39.565
and what you're going to do.

00:52:39.565 --> 00:52:40.690
Together.

00:52:40.690 --> 00:52:41.410
Together!

00:52:41.410 --> 00:52:43.315
[GIGGLING]

00:52:43.315 --> 00:52:47.290
Julie, you and I.

00:52:47.290 --> 00:52:50.080
We're going to do something
together, we've been saying.

00:52:50.080 --> 00:52:51.220
Yes, yes.

00:52:51.220 --> 00:52:54.950
Yes, let's do that.

00:52:54.950 --> 00:52:57.860
Thank you so much,
thank you for having us.

00:52:57.860 --> 00:52:58.758
We love you.

00:52:58.758 --> 00:52:59.800
Thank you for doing this.

00:52:59.800 --> 00:53:05.770
You all are both so busy, and
so famous, and so fabulous.

00:53:05.770 --> 00:53:06.730
It's true.

00:53:06.730 --> 00:53:09.670
And I feel honored to have
been able to talk to you

00:53:09.670 --> 00:53:12.320
and to have this conversation
between the two of you

00:53:12.320 --> 00:53:13.930
that can always
be on the record.

00:53:13.930 --> 00:53:16.600
What a thrill for
people who love film.

00:53:16.600 --> 00:53:18.560
Love you both.

00:53:18.560 --> 00:53:21.150
Hi, my name is a Ava
DuVernay, and for more videos,

00:53:21.150 --> 00:53:23.920
go to the Oscars channel.

